# Code Quality — Always-On Rules

Optimise for the next reader. "It works" is not the quality bar: if the same behaviour is reachable with a clearer structure, choose the clearer one — direct and boring beats clever. These rules are language- and product-agnostic.

## Scope discipline

- Match refactor ambition to the task. Bugfix and small-feature tasks get minimal diffs; structural reframes ("this branch/layer could disappear") are *proposed* in one short paragraph, not implemented — unless the task is explicitly a refactor.
- Never restructure code unrelated to the current change.

## Simplicity & control flow

- Do not bolt one-off special cases onto unrelated paths. A repeated conditional on the same discriminator signals a missing model or helper.
- Introduce a typed model / dispatcher only when cases are open-ended or already ≥3. A 2–3 case if-chain stays an if-chain.

```js
// ❌ BAD: special case smuggled into an unrelated path
function render(item) {
  if (item.kind === "legacy" && item.flagX && !item.hidden) { /* one-off */ }
  // ...
}

// ✅ GOOD: dispatch on a typed model (cases are open-ended)
const renderers = { legacy: renderLegacy, modern: renderModern };
renderers[item.kind](item);
```

## Abstraction & boundaries

Two kinds of abstraction, two different bars:

- **Boundary abstractions** (interfaces in front of external services, I/O, or swappable strategies — LLM providers, OCR engines, storage): justified with even a single implementation. The point is isolating a volatile dependency, not deduplication. Callers depend on the interface, never on a concrete implementation or vendor SDK.
- **Internal indirection** (helpers, wrappers, pass-through layers inside a module): justified only at ≥2 real call sites or when it makes an invariant type-safe. Otherwise inline it.
- Keep logic in its canonical layer: feature logic stays out of shared paths, implementation details stay behind the interface.
- Before writing a helper, check whether a canonical one already exists.

## Modularity & file size

- Split proactively, not when the linter complains: separate orchestration from business logic; one focused responsibility per file/module.
- Growing an existing file substantially is a smell — extract helpers, sub-components, or modules *first*, then add the feature.
- Hard limits (`max-lines`, complexity) live in the lint config. Never bypass them with disable comments — restructure instead.

## Types & state

- Question needless optionality and silent fallbacks; make invariants explicit in the types (`any` is lint-enforced, not negotiable).
- Do not introduce concurrency/parallelism without naming the race and ordering implications in the change description. Keep related state updates atomic — no half-applied intermediate states.

## When NOT to apply

Hotfixes under time pressure, vendored/generated code, and tasks with an explicit minimal-diff requirement: fix only what was asked, note observed smells in the summary instead.

## Final check (before the final diff summary)

Answer these explicitly before presenting the result:

- [ ] No special-case branch added to an unrelated flow.
- [ ] No new *internal* abstraction without ≥2 call sites or a type-safety win; boundary interfaces wrap volatile/external dependencies only.
- [ ] Logic lives in its canonical layer; existing helpers reused.
- [ ] No lint limit bypassed via disable comment.
- [ ] Any structural reframe was proposed, not silently implemented.
